-- title:
--   An odd power distinguishes the two directed Cayley traces
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite abelian group and $a,d,h\in G$. Suppose $2h=0$, $h\ne0$, the image of $d$ has additive order exactly three in $Q=G/\langle h\rangle$, the subgroup generated by $a,h$ is all of $G$, and $|Q|$ is odd. Let $A_+,A_-$ be the rational directed Cayley adjacency matrices for supports $\{a,a+d,a+d+h\}$ and $\{a,a-d,a-d+h\}$. Then $$\exists n\in\mathbb N,\quad\operatorname{tr}(A_+^{2n+1})\ne\operatorname{tr}(A_-^{2n+1}).$$ This gives an odd positive discriminating degree; it does not assert that the degree is the first discrepancy. Directed arcs and any loops retain their original meaning.
-- source:
--   Alexandre Couret, https://github.com/alexcour/couret-finite-verifications/blob/65b828d9ffd016ce2a1b05403f303fa5f948c10f/research/correspondants_20261013/lean/CayleyTwoColourNecessity.lean#L105-L119, lines 105–119; source file SHA-256 7675ca2a1f08e8c6a20617d181810b5c2b6a4f0cffcf96b93678cb0bc9c7869d; theorem Couret.CayleyDoublet.abelianThree_odd_trace_witness_of_odd_quotient

